      "text": "To find the volume of the frustum, we first need to determine the volume of the original pyramid and the volume of the smaller pyramid that is cut away. The volume \\(V\\) of a pyramid is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height} \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the volume of the original pyramid. The base of the original pyramid is a square with side length 12 cm, so the base area is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Base Area}_{\\text{original}} = 12^2 = 144 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nThe height of the original pyramid is 8 cm. Therefore, the volume of the original pyramid is:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 \\times 8 = 384 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the volume of the smaller pyramid. The base of the smaller pyramid is a square with side length 6 cm, so the base area is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Base Area}_{\\text{smaller}} = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nThe height of the smaller pyramid is 4 cm. Therefore, the volume of the smaller pyramid is:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{smaller}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 36 \\times 4 = 48 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nThe volume of the frustum is the volume of the original pyramid minus the volume of the smaller pyramid:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{frustum}} = V_{\\text{original}} - V_{\\text{smaller}} = 384 - 48 = 336 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nThus, the volume of the frustum is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{336} \\]",
